$DIR/main.p:5:3: defer inside a loop is not supported in Plutonium (it would need unbounded stack); move the loop body into a function
    A defer statement inside a loop would add a deferred call on every
    iteration, and each needs room in the function's stack frame until it
    returns. Plutonium gives every deferred call a fixed slot in the frame, so
    the number must be known when compiling.
    Move the loop body into a function of its own; its defer then runs at the
    end of each iteration.
    Go stores such deferred calls on the heap. Plutonium never allocates behind
    the program's back, and kernel stacks are small and fixed.
    Move the body into a function:
        for _, name := range names {
            process(name)
        }

        func process(name string) {
            f := open(name)
            defer f.Close()
            ...
        }
    (see "Defer statements" in the Go spec; Plutonium adds this limit)
    (run "pluto explain E2530" for a longer explanation)
